        "title": "Keeping your books",
        "summary": "Record every sale and every payment on the day it happens.",
        "cards": [
            {
                "front": "Books",
                "back": "Your books are the record of every franc that comes in and goes out of the business, written in one place."
            },
            {
                "front": "Same day",
                "back": "Write each sale and each payment on the day it happens. Records written later are where gaps begin."
            },
            {
                "front": "Cash sales",
                "back": "Cash sales are the easiest to forget. Count the till at the close of each day and record the total."
            },
            {
                "front": "Receipts",
                "back": "Keep a receipt or invoice for every cost. A cost without a receipt is hard for your Audit Partner to verify."
            },
            {
                "front": "One record per transaction",
                "back": "Each line says the date, the amount, who paid or was paid, and what it was for."
            },
            {
                "front": "Business and personal",
                "back": "Money you take for yourself is \"paid to owners\". Record it as its own line, never as a business cost."
            },
            {
                "front": "Monthly close",
                "back": "At the end of each month, add up money in, costs by category and money paid to owners."
            },
            {
                "front": "Your Record Gap",
                "back": "Each month your Audit Partner compares your figures with what they verify. The difference is your Record Gap."
            },
            {
                "front": "Templates",
                "back": "You do not need software to keep good books. A notebook or a spreadsheet kept every day is enough."
            }
        ],
